# bug-nilpy-python-import-resolves-against-c-headers
#
# The guard for a REORDERING, so the row that matters is what must NOT change.
#
# `import <c-header>` is a DESIGNED NilPy feature — the wrapper-free NilPy-to-C
# arc. An earlier attempt at this ticket gated the /usr/include route on
# `not isNilPy` and turned four tests red, because the ticket's premise ("import
# stdio must be no module named stdio") was simply wrong. The route stays; only
# its POSITION moved, so that a Python stdlib name which also names a C header
# (string, math) reaches the Python module first instead of losing to the
# header.
#
# So this file imports C headers from a .npy and uses them, exactly as the
# feature intends. If the reordering ever turns into a removal again, this goes
# red immediately rather than four unrelated-looking tests going red later.
#
# The sqlite and c-pointer tests cover the same property from other angles —
# and two of those live in test-core, not test-nilpy, which is the process
# lesson this ticket recorded: a change to the IMPORT RESOLVER is a test-core
# change too, whatever frontend it looks like it belongs to.

import stdlib
import stdio

p = malloc(64)
free(p)
print("malloc/free ok")
print("abs        ", abs(-3))
